Chapter III - Anomaly  

1 Billion Years Later, At somewhere in space. * 

The console room hummed with the low, contented sigh of a TARDIS at rest — not idling, exactly, more like a great sleeping animal breathing between adventures. Clara Oswald sat cross-legged on the jump seat, wringing seawater out of the hem of her cardigan, while the Doctor circled the console barefoot, leaving wet footprints that the ship seemed to be quietly, disapprovingly evaporating as fast as he made them.  

"I'm just saying," Clara said, "a planet that's entirely ocean. No coastline. No beach. No little umbrella in your drink because there's nowhere to put a bar."  

"There was a floating bar."  

"There was a raft with a fish on it who mixed cocktails with his tail."  

"Best mixologist in three galaxies, that fish." The Doctor flicked a switch, frowned at the readout it gave him, and flicked it back. "You're just annoyed you lost the swimming race."  

"I was winning the swimming race, until something the size of a bus decided I looked like lunch."  

"You looked delicious. I told you, that colour suits you." He grinned at her sideways, the kind of grin that always meant he was proud of himself for something only he found funny yet. "Besides, technically you didn't lose. You achieved a highly efficient tactical retreat."  

"I screamed and climbed you like a tree."  

"Like I said. Tactical." He straightened up, wiped his hands on his tweed — which did absolutely nothing, since his hands were still faintly turquoise from the planet's bioluminescent shallows — and beamed around the console room as if it were the finest view in the universe, which, depending on the day, it occasionally was. "Best fully-oceanic planet in the whole of the Local Group, that one. No name yet, as it happens. Native life hasn't invented one. Bit rude of us to have visited a whole world and not even know what to call it, really."  

"So name it."  

"Can't. Rules." He said this the way he said most rules — as though they were both sacred and enormously fun to have just broken. "Naming rights go to the first species to develop written language. They're only up to interpretive bubble-patterns at the moment. Give it another eleven million years."  

Clara opened her mouth to say something about patience not being either of their strong suits, when a sound cut across the console room — small, tinny, entirely wrong for a time machine the size of a cathedral inside a police box. A ring. An old-fashioned, insistent, brring-brring sort of ring, coming from the ancient black telephone bolted to the console, the one Clara had always assumed was decorative, in the way that everything on the TARDIS was decorative right up until the moment it very much wasn't.  

The Doctor went still.  

"Is that—"  

"It's the phone." He said it quietly, almost reverently, already moving.  

"I didn't know it actually worked."  

"Everything works. Eventually. That's rather the point of it." He picked up the receiver, and for a moment his whole face changed — the easy grin folding away into something older, more careful, the face of a man doing sums no one else could see. He didn't say hello. He never said hello to this particular caller; Clara would learn that soon enough.  

"Yes," the Doctor said instead, listening, nodding once at nothing, his knuckles pale around the receiver. A pause. Longer this time. His eyes flicked, just once, to Clara, and away again. "Yes. I understand. Hold tight — don't do anything until we get there."  

He set the phone down with a click that seemed far too loud for such a small, ordinary sound.  

"Doctor?" Clara was on her feet now, cardigan forgotten. "Who was that?"  

He was already at the console, already throwing levers, already wearing the particular expression he wore right before the floor started shaking and the world outside the doors stopped being safe. The great central column began to rise and fall, and the ancient engines groaned their familiar, impossible groan.  

"Old friend," he said. "Rather insistent one. Tends to be, when there's a war on."  

"A war? Whose war?"  

The Doctor didn't answer straightaway. He was too busy grinning again — but it was a different grin now, sharper at the edges, the one that meant he'd already decided this was going to be enormous, and possibly on fire.  

"Hold onto something, Clara," he said instead. "We're going to see Winston Churchill!"  

"The Winston Churchill?" Clara said, disbelief written all over her face.  

The Doctor just smiled and flipped the dematerialisation switch. The ground started shaking, and the TARDIS started up that humming noise — that deep, groaning, wheezing sound that meant the universe was about to be rearranged around them, whether it liked it or not.  

Questions started flowing through Clara's head, and she began asking them rapidly. How does Winston Churchill have your number? Does he know about the TARDIS? How did you two even meet?  

"I met him a long time ago," the Doctor said. "He's a friend" — not answering any of Clara's questions.  

The central column slowed its pumping, the wheezing groan deepening into something lower, heavier, almost tired — and then came the sound Clara had already learned to brace for: a series of scraping, shuddering thuds, like the TARDIS was dragging its heels on the way down. The lights along the console dimmed once, flickered, and steadied.  

They'd landed.  

The Doctor was already straightening his bow tie in the nearest reflective surface, looking entirely too pleased with himself for a man who'd just refused to answer three perfectly reasonable questions.  

"Well," he said, striding for the doors. "Best behaviour, Clara. Difficult year for old Winston, this one."  

"Which year?"  

He paused, hand on the door, and glanced back at her with that maddening half-smile.  

"Come and find out."  

He threw the doors open.  

Clara started walking rapidly toward them, excitement written all over her face — the kind of excitement that came with every single landing, no matter how many times she told herself she was used to it by now. She stopped dead in the doorway.  

They were standing on top of a building, wind cutting sharp and cold across the rooftop, and below them sprawled a city she almost didn't recognise. 1939, the Doctor had as good as said. Britain. And spread out beneath her in the grey light was mile after mile of wreckage — collapsed roofs, blackened brick, streets half-swallowed by rubble, the whole skyline scarred in a way no history book photograph had ever quite prepared her for.  

"Doctor," she said quietly, all the earlier questions gone out of her voice. "What happened here?"  

"War," the Doctor said, his face suddenly sad.  

He turned and started down a narrow flight of stairs without another word. Clara followed him, as she always did.  

When they reached the bottom, the place was chaos — hundreds of people talking over one another, papers changing hands, a current of panic running under everything like a live wire. This was the command centre of the war.  

The Doctor and Clara started making their way across the chaos, weaving between desks and running aides, and it was clear he was searching for someone. Clara guessed it was probably Churchill. They searched for three or four minutes — hardly any time at all, really — but to Clara it felt like a great deal longer, the noise and the panic stretching every second thin.  

Finally they found him. He had a big, twirly moustache, a black and long hat, and a fat Cuban cigar clamped in the corner of his mouth. The moment he saw the Doctor, his whole face lit up. He plucked the cigar from his lips and said:  

"Doctor. It's not going well here. They've surrounded us from all sides."  

"You know I can't, Winston," the Doctor said, his face full of apology.  

"I know, I know — we're just trying to hold on to something here. But that's not why I called you." He started walking through the chaos of the room with an ease that seemed entirely out of place, and continued, "I called you because there's something not normal here. Something... something you, I suppose."  

The Doctor and Clara exchanged a curious glance as they followed him.  

Churchill made his way to a wall in the middle of the room, reached into his jacket pocket, and pulled out a key. Clara wondered why he was standing in front of a plain stretch of wall — was there a secret door?  

There was. Churchill leaned his weight against it, and the wall began to move. Clara glanced around; everyone in the room was far too busy to notice three people slipping through a wall that apparently wasn't a wall at all. Or maybe, she thought, they already knew it was there.  

They stepped through, and there was another door waiting on the other side. Churchill worked the lock with his key and pushed it open, already moving through it as he said, "Come on, hurry up!"  

Beyond it was a thin, unlit corridor, with a set of stairs descending into darkness at the far end. At the bottom of the stairs was a small, unlit square room. They stepped inside — and there it was. The reason Churchill had called the Doctor.  

Stretched across the middle of the room, from one wall to the other, was something that didn't quite belong to any category Clara had words for. A kind of invisible wall, opaque and shimmering, like a sheet of liquid standing upright — and it was moving, slowly, rippling in on itself the way water does when nothing is meant to be disturbing it.  

The Doctor looked at it with that curious face of his, the one he always wore right before something got interesting, and started making strange rubbing motions in the air near it — never quite touching it. Amazement crept across his face, and he whispered, "A space-time crack."  

Churchill looked on like a proud father watching his child take its first steps.  

"So what does it do? Why is it here?" Clara said, excitement filling her voice.  

"It's a crack in the fabric of space and time," the Doctor said, still inspecting it closely. "It can open onto anywhere in space, at any point in time."  

"So it goes somewhere? Like a portal?"  

"Exactly. Anywhere. Anytime."  

And with that, he jumped straight into the anomaly and vanished.  

Clara didn't hesitate — with the same excitement still lighting up her face, she jumped in right behind him.  

Churchill sighed, tucked his cigar back between his lips, and returned to his work as though nothing at all had happened. As though it were just another Tuesday. 

When Clara climbed out of the crack, she found the Doctor already standing a few paces off, looking out at the horizon. 

The planet was covered in sand. Not dunes here and there, not a stretch of desert between two greener things — sand all the way out, in every direction, until the ground and the sky agreed to stop arguing about where one ended. 

"This planet feels similar," the Doctor said. "Maybe even familiar." He was clearly running through strange scenarios in his head. 

Clara turned to him, her face troubled. "How come?" 

The Doctor stuck his finger in his mouth, then held it up in front of him the way he'd hold the sonic screwdriver. Except it was just his finger. He seemed to be calculating something. 

"I feel like I've been here," he said. "But I can't quite put my finger on it." 

He smiled at his own joke. 

"Oh — also, the city's that way." He said it with complete confidence, pointing off into the emptiness with the same finger he'd just licked. 

Clara stared at him, mouth slightly open, trying to work out how on earth he could possibly know where a city was. 

While Clara was still trying to work out how the Doctor knew where the city was, she started to hear a sound — a buzzing, somewhere out in all that sand. An engine, she thought, or something close to one. 

A man appeared out of nowhere. A bandana across his forehead, enormous sand goggles over his eyes, a mask covering the lower half of his face — and beneath him, a motorcycle. Not an ordinary one, either. It hovered a hand's width above the sand without ever quite touching it. 

He pulled up close and cut the engine. 

"Hey. You two new here?" he said, tugging the mask down off his face. 

"Yeah," the Doctor said. "Could you take us to the city?" 

The stranger shrugged. "Sure. Get on." There wasn't a trace of worry anywhere on his face. 

The Doctor and Clara climbed onto the back of the motorcycle, and he set off. 

For the first minute or so, nobody said anything. The bike made a low, steady hum, and the sand went by underneath them in a pale blur, and Clara held on with both hands and tried not to think about how fast the horizon was arriving. 

"So," the Doctor said, leaning forward, having to raise his voice over the engine. "Do you stop for everyone out here, or are we special?" 

"Nobody's out here," the stranger called back. "That's how I knew you were new. People who know this place don't stand about in the open like that." 

"Why not?" 

"Because of the heat." 

There was a pause, exactly one beat too long. 

"Right," the Doctor said. "The heat." 

Clara caught it too. She glanced sideways at him, and he gave her the smallest shake of his head — not now — and went straight back to being cheerful. 

"Lovely bike, this. Antigrav suspension?" 

"Something like that." 

"Only, the field's about two hundred years ahead of the paint job." The Doctor patted the chassis with something like affection. "Which is a very interesting thing for a bike to be." 

The stranger didn't answer that. 

"What's your name?" Clara asked. 

"Doesn't matter." 

"It usually does." 

"Not here." He said it flatly, without any rudeness in it, the way you'd tell someone the shops shut at six. 

Clara looked out across the sand instead. There was nothing. No road, no marker, no track behind them — the bike didn't leave one, which she supposed was the point of hovering. Just the same emptiness in every direction, going on and on. 

"Can I ask you something?" she said. "How do you know which way you're going? There's nothing out here." 

"Same way he does," the stranger said, and jerked his chin back at the Doctor without turning round. 

Clara turned to look at the Doctor. The Doctor was smiling, but it wasn't reaching his eyes at all. 

They drove for five, maybe ten minutes. Then the shape of it came up out of the haze ahead — low buildings, sand-coloured, half-buried, and above them a scatter of towers that caught the light in a way that sand does not. 

The bike slowed and settled, and the hum died away. 

"This is you," the stranger said. 

They climbed off. Clara's legs felt strange under her, the way they do after any journey where you've been holding on the whole time. 

"Thank you," she said. "Really." 

"Don't wander at night." He was already pulling his mask back up. "And don't tell anyone how you got here." 

"Why not?" said the Doctor. 

The stranger looked at him for a long moment through those enormous goggles. It was impossible to read anything at all in his face. 

"Because you don't have the accent," he said. "And people notice." 

Then he twisted the throttle, and the bike lifted, and he was gone back out into the sand — no trail, no dust, nothing to say he'd ever been there at all. 

Clara watched the empty horizon where he'd been. 

"Doctor," she said. "That man knew exactly who we were." 

"Oh, he knew a great deal more than that." The Doctor was still staring after him, hands in his pockets. "He knew we'd ask for the city. He didn't ask which one." 

He turned towards the buildings, and the grin came back — the sharp one. 

"Come on, Clara. Let's go and find out what everyone here is being so calm about." 

They came into the city along what seemed to be the main street. Buildings ran away to the left and right of them — some of them clearly expensive, with clean new-looking walls, and some of them old. Old old. Painted in strange, unique colours that had faded into one another over a very long time. 

As they walked, people watched them. Not one or two. Everyone. Every person on that street, and every animal too, turning to follow them with their eyes. 

Clara looked at the Doctor. He didn't seem remotely disturbed by it. She had the distinct impression he was rather enjoying it. 

Then a child started walking towards them, and the Doctor slowed. 

The kid came right up, stopped, and said, "Follow me." Then he turned and walked back the way he'd come. 

They followed. It was the most curious either of them had been in a week, and that was saying something. 

The child led them to a short brick building — plainly an important one, judging by the official-looking titles carved into the wall beside the door. They went inside. Clara glanced around as they crossed the hall, still wondering why a child had walked up to two total strangers in the street and told them where to go. 

They went up a spiral staircase made of wood. 

There was a man waiting at the top, in a room whose door had MAYOR written on it. They walked in. He was standing at the window with his back to them, looking down onto the main street. 

He turned around. 

"Hello, strangers," he said. "I'm the Mayor of this city. How can I help?" 

The Doctor had a bad feeling. 

Something was bothering him — or rather, someone. It didn't show on his face. Even Clara couldn't see it. But something was wrong. 

After a two-second pause, he started talking. 

"Hey, Mayo! What's wrong with your planet?" he asked, with a curious look. 

The Mayor hadn't been expecting either half of that, and took both as somewhat rude. 

"What do you mean, what's wrong with my planet?" There was a harshness in his voice now. 

The Doctor paused, turned to his right, and started walking. He had entered his special teaching mode. 

"There was a crack in the fabric of space and time. We went into it on Earth, in 1941, and we came out here. At..." He stopped pacing for half a second. "Where even is this place?" 

"I named this planet when I found it. I'm the founder of this planet." The Mayor smiled. "It's called Skaro. Nice name, isn't it?" 

The Doctor stopped. 

He turned to the Mayor with a stunned look on his face. 

"What did you say?" 

"Skaro. The planet is called Skaro." The Mayor was trying to work out why the Doctor had given him that response. 

"Skaro. The Skaro?" The Doctor's voice had gone strange. "But — isn't there a war going on?" 

"A war? What war? There's no war here. We live in peace." 

"Oh..." 

He tried to get his energy back. 

"Sorry. Then we haven't met properly, have we? I'm the Doctor." The charming grin returned as though it had never left. 

"Good to meet you." They shook hands. "And this is my son." The Mayor pointed at the child they'd followed here. The kid smiled. 

"Can I offer you something?" 

"Tea?" the Doctor asked. 

"Tea." 

They started walking towards the far end of the long, rectangular room. Clara and the Mayor's son stayed near the door, watching them go. 

And as the Doctor and the Mayor walked, the invisible wall reappeared. 

The same one from Earth. The same slow, shimmering, liquid nothing. 

It took them both. They were pulled in and gone in less than a second. 

Clara and the boy stood frozen in shock. 

Then they ran — both of them, without a word between them — and threw themselves in after them. 

When the two of them came out of the crack, Clara and the boy looked at each other. 

Because there was no sign of the Doctor. And no sign of his father. 

They were somewhere else entirely. Somewhere with trees. 

A forest. A real one — proper trees with proper leaves on them, and green light coming down through the canopy, and the smell of damp earth. After a whole planet of sand it was almost offensive. 

Clara turned in a slow circle. No path. No buildings. No crack, either — the thing had closed behind them without leaving so much as a shimmer. 

"Right," she said, mostly to herself. "Of course it has." 

The boy hadn't moved at all. 

He wasn't looking for his father. He wasn't looking for the crack. He was looking up, with his head tipped all the way back and his mouth slightly open, at a hundred feet of green. 

"Hey," Clara said. "Are you—" 

"What are they?" 

She stopped. "...Trees?" 

"Trees." He said the word carefully, the way you handle something you've only ever read about. He put a hand flat against the nearest trunk, then pulled it back, then put it there again. "It's wet. Why is it wet?" 

"That's — that's just how they are. There's water in them." 

"There's water in them." He looked at his palm. He looked at the tree. He looked at the several thousand other trees. "There's water in all of them." 

Clara understood, then, and felt slightly sick about how long it had taken her. 

Sand. All the way to the horizon, in every direction, with nothing in it. 

"You've never seen a forest," she said. 

"I've never seen a plant." He crouched down and touched the moss at the base of the trunk with one finger, very lightly, like it might object. "We have pictures. In the archive. My father says there were green places once, a long time ago, before the — " He stopped himself. Straightened up. Put his face back on. "It doesn't matter." 

Before the what? Clara thought. But he'd shut the door on it, and she'd been travelling long enough to know when not to shove. 

"It matters a bit," she said instead. "You're standing in the first forest you've ever seen. Most people would take a minute." 

So he took a minute. 

He walked between the trunks with his head back and his arms out, brushing his fingertips along the bark, breathing in air that smelled of rain and rot and living things. He found a fern and crouched over it for a long time. He picked up a leaf and turned it over twice and put it in his pocket. Something in his face had come loose that had been screwed down tight since the street, and he was — Clara realised, watching him — completely, ordinarily happy. 

For about ninety seconds, the boy forgot everything. 

Then he stopped. 

She saw the exact moment it came back to him. It went across his face like a shadow crossing a field. 

"My father," he said. 

"Hey—" 

He spun round, looking for the crack — for anything, a shimmer, a seam in the air — and there was nothing behind them but more trees, identical in every direction, and no way at all to tell which patch of empty forest they'd fallen out of. 

"It was there. It was right there, it was—" His voice climbed. "Where is it? Where's the wall? It has to still be—" 

"It's closed. It closed behind us." 

"Then open it." 

"I can't." 

"He could." The boy's hands were shaking. He shoved them under his arms so she wouldn't see. "The man with you. He'd know how. And he's gone too, isn't he, so that's — so nobody knows. Nobody knows where they went and nobody knows how to follow and I was looking at leaves." 

That last one came out badly. He heard it come out badly. 

Clara crouched down so she wasn't standing over him. 

"Listen to me. You didn't do anything wrong by being amazed at a tree." She waited until he looked at her. "And they're not gone. They went through the same crack we did, about four seconds ahead of us. That's all. Four seconds." 

"Then why aren't they here?" 

"Because those things don't put you down neatly, they just — put you down." She stood, and held out a hand. "Come on. Walking's better than standing." 

He didn't take her hand, but he walked. 

They went in the direction that seemed least like the others, which was the only method Clara had. The forest floor was soft. Somewhere above them, something was making a noise that was almost, but not quite, birdsong. 

"So what was that?" the boy asked eventually. "That thing. The wall." 

"A crack in time. In space and time, actually — the Doctor explained it and I understood about a third of it, which is a good day for me." She ducked under a branch. "It opens onto somewhere else. Anywhere else. And apparently it doesn't ask first." 

"Where does it go?" 

"Everywhere, from what I can tell. I came out of one on a rooftop in 1941 and ended up on your planet, so." She shrugged. "Your guess is as good as mine." 

"That's not an answer." 

"You sound like him." 

The boy considered this and didn't seem to mind it. 

"What do you think happened to them?" he said. "My father and your... " He hesitated. "What is he?" 

"That," Clara said, "is a very good question, and nobody's got a proper answer to it yet, including him." She thought about it. "He's my friend. He's the cleverest person I've ever met and he's very bad at explaining himself, and if there's a way out of somewhere, he'll find it about four minutes before anyone else notices there's a problem. So — my honest guess? They came out somewhere. Maybe together. Maybe not. And he's already working on getting back." 

"And if he isn't?" 

"Then we find them ourselves." She said it lightly, which took some doing. "That's the job. It's mostly walking, if I'm honest." 

They went on for a while. 

"I don't know your name," Clara said. "I've just dragged you through a hole in the universe and I don't know your name. Bit rude of me." 

The boy pushed a low branch out of his way and held it for her, which she thought was rather nicely done for someone who'd just lost his father. 

"Davros," he said.

"Davros." Clara smiled at him. It was a nice name. She'd never heard it before. "I'm Clara."

And they kept walking, deeper into the trees.

After a while, they began to realise the size of the place. It didn't end. Whichever way they went, the forest arranged itself into the same scenery — the same spacing of trunks, the same green light, the same not-quite-birdsong somewhere overhead.

Clara didn't want to look hopeless. She didn't want to frighten him.

Davros turned to her anyway.

"We've been walking a long time," he said. "And we haven't got anywhere."

Clara looked down at him.

"There's always a way out. And the Doctor would never leave me behind. We'll get out of here somehow."

To take the weight out of the air, she started asking him things.

"So it was your dad who found that planet, was it? Why start a whole new life out there — was his own planet not good enough for him?" She pulled a face at him, mock-serious.

"My father had to flee his home world a long time ago. He says there was a great war there. A huge one." Davros stepped over a root. "He doesn't talk about his past much. I think it brings his fears back."

They kept walking.

The deeper they went, the quieter it got. The not-quite-birdsong thinned out and stopped. Even their own footsteps seemed to be arriving from further away than they should.

Then they saw it.

A crab.

Clara stopped short, surprised — a crab, here, miles from any water she could see.

Davros didn't react the way she did at all.

He froze.

His eyes locked onto the thing, and for several seconds he said nothing whatsoever.

Clara looked at him. There was something strange in his face. Almost as though he recognised it.

Almost as though it recognised him.

Davros started slowly towards it.

"Davros — careful. Crabs can hurt you. Don't get too close."

But he'd already picked it up. The creature sat perfectly calm in his hands, claws folded, not struggling in the slightest.

Clara took a step back from him without entirely deciding to.

She turned away and started looking for another route through the trees. After seven or eight paces she glanced back over her shoulder.

"Don't fall behind. This place might be dangerous."

"Yes, yes. I'm coming," Davros said. He was still looking at the crab.

She turned round and carried on walking. She walked, and walked, and then she heard a whisper.

It was coming from a tree.

She approached it slowly. The whisper got louder, and then she recognised the voice.

"Clara... help me..."

That was Davros. But Davros was supposed to be right behind her.

Clara spun round. "DAVROS!"

No answer. She looked everywhere, and he was gone — she had walked away from him without noticing, without any idea how long ago, or how far.

Then the voice came again. "Clara... help me..."

That was enough. Davros was in trouble. Clara ran at the tree, and without stopping to think about what she was doing, she jumped straight through it.

And then — darkness. Absolute darkness.

Clara turned slowly on the spot. Nothing. No trees. No Davros. No sound at all. She was alone.

Then she saw it: a faint blue glow, somewhere out in all that black.

The TARDIS. It stood there in silence, wrapped in that familiar bluish light, exactly as it always had. For the first time since she'd entered the forest, Clara felt safe.

She ran to it. She reached the door. She knocked four times.

Then she waited. Ten seconds. Twenty. Thirty.

Finally, the door opened.

Clara looked inside — and froze.

The man standing there wasn't the Doctor she knew. There was no bow tie. Instead there was messy hair, a long brown coat, and a face full of impossible energy.

Clara couldn't speak.

She hadn't found her Doctor. She'd found another one.

The Tenth Doctor.

Elsewhere. Across space and time. * 

The crack put them down without ceremony.

That was the trouble with cracks in the fabric of everything. They had no manners at all. They took you mid-step, mid-sentence, mid-thought, and they set you down somewhere else entirely with your foot still coming down on a floor that had stopped existing several billion miles ago.

The Doctor's foot came down on sand.

He kept going anyway — three more paces, arms out, coat-tails swinging, the way you walk when a staircase has one fewer step in it than you were promised — and then he stopped, and looked at his shoes, and looked up.

Sand.

Sand in a full circle around him, flat and pale and going on and on, with a horizon drawn round the edge of it like a line somebody had ruled with a very long ruler.

"Oh," he said. "Well. That's cheating."

Because at first glance it was Skaro. The same washed-out gold. The same enormous silence. The same sky the colour of weak tea, with no sun anywhere in it and light coming from everywhere regardless, which was a trick he had meant to ask somebody about.

At second glance it was not.

He crouched, and took up a handful, and let it go in a thin stream out of the side of his fist, and watched how it fell.

It fell wrong.

Not dramatically. Nothing so useful as dramatically. It simply came down a fraction too slowly and landed a fraction too evenly, and the little cone it made at his feet had sides that were the same on every side, and sand does not do that. Sand is lazy and various and full of opinions. This sand had been to school.

"Hello," he said to it, quietly, in the voice he used for things that were pretending. "And what are you, then?"

He straightened up and turned round, once, all the way, taking it in properly.

No wind. That was the first thing. Not a breath of it, and yet the surface had ripples on it, neat parallel ripples running away from him in every direction at once, which is a thing wind does and nothing else does, and there was no wind.

No tracks. That was the second thing, and it was worse, because there were two of them standing there and they had walked four paces each and there ought to have been eight footprints behind them. There were. He counted them. Then he looked again a moment later and there were six, and then four, and the sand was closing them up behind him with the unhurried patience of a thing tidying its own house.

The Mayor stood where the crack had left him, hands loose at his sides, waiting.

Somewhere on the other side of all that nothing, Clara was — and here the Doctor's mind did what it always did, which was to open the drawer marked CLARA, look directly into it, and shut it again very firmly. She had gone through four seconds behind him. She had a good head and an appalling sense of self-preservation and she was with a frightened child, which meant she would be twice as sensible as usual, because she always was when somebody smaller needed her to be. She would be walking. She would be talking. She would be fine.

She would be fine, and he would find her, and the way to do the second thing was to stop standing about in a desert admiring the quality of the sand.

The Mayor spoke.

"How are we going to get back?"

He asked it the way you'd ask a man at a bus stop about the timetable. No fear in it. No hurry. Only a mild, practical interest in the answer.

The Doctor put a finger to his lips.

Then he lifted it, straight up, high above his head, and held it there, and shut one eye.

He stayed like that for rather a long time.

"Are you," the Mayor said eventually, "measuring the wind?"

"There isn't any wind."

"I know."

"That's what I'm measuring." The Doctor kept his finger where it was. "You can learn an enormous amount from a thing that isn't happening. People never ask what isn't happening. It's always what's that noise, never what's that silence, and the silence is nearly always the more interesting of the two."

He brought his hand down, frowned at his own finger as though it had let him down personally, and reached into his coat.

The sonic screwdriver came out with a flourish it did not strictly need.

He swept it through the air in a slow arc, left to right, the green light at its tip pulsing and stuttering — and then he stopped, and turned it round, and held the thing up against his ear like a shell, with his head tilted and his eyes almost closed, listening to it the way you'd listen to somebody very old telling you something very important in a very quiet voice.

The sonic whispered. The Doctor listened.

Then he nodded, once.

"Five minutes."

The Mayor waited.

"In five minutes," the Doctor said, "a crack will open. Directly ahead of us. About a hundred metres out, give or take the width of a small dog." He put the screwdriver away. "We walk through it, and out we go, and off we jolly well pop."

He did not say the rest of it, because the rest of it had arrived in his head uninvited and was sitting there being unwelcome.

Five minutes was too tidy. Cracks did not run to a timetable. They were wounds, not doors — they came when the wall was weak and they went when it healed, and asking one to turn up in five minutes at a hundred metres was like asking a bruise to appear on a Thursday. And yet there it was, ticking away out in the empty air ahead of him, as punctual as a train.

It was as though all of this had been arranged a very long time ago, and they were only now getting round to the part with him in it.

"Okay," the Mayor said.

The Doctor turned his head.

That was all. Okay. A man had told him that a hole was going to open in the universe in five minutes, and he had said okay, and he had said it in the tone of a man being told the tea would be another few moments.

No surprise. No questions. Not one.

The Doctor had spent nine hundred years watching people meet the impossible, and there were only ever four ways it went. They screamed, or they laughed, or they fainted, or — best of all, his favourite, the reason he kept coming back — they asked how. Nobody said okay. Okay was what you said about things you had already known.

That bothered him.

So he had five minutes. He decided to spend them.

"Why did you name the planet Skaro?"

The Mayor frowned. "Why does that matter?"

The Doctor didn't answer. He was already moving — a slow half-circle round the man, hands in his pockets, apparently fascinated by the horizon over his shoulder — and as he came round the second time he brought the sonic out low and quick and ran it up the length of him, wrist to crown, in a single pass.

The device hummed. The Doctor glanced down at what it told him.

His eyes narrowed.

He looked at the reading. He looked at the Mayor. He looked at the reading again, and gave it a small shake, which had never once worked on any piece of equipment in his entire life and which he did anyway.

Unknown.

Not unregistered. Not corrupted, not out of range, not any of the forty-one polite ways his screwdriver had of saying it was having a difficult afternoon. Unknown. A blank space where a species ought to be, on a device that had catalogued every living thing between here and the Medusa Cascade and had, on one memorable occasion, correctly identified a sentient hat.

"Why did you point that thing at my face?" the Mayor asked.

The Doctor made it vanish into his coat with the speed of a man palming a card.

"Nothing important. Forget it."

"Your expression says otherwise."

"My expression says a great many things. It's a very talkative face. Best to ignore about eighty per cent of it."

But the smile had gone off him, and they both knew it.

Because there were only two ways to be unknown, and he had turned them both over already, twice, while he was busy pretending to look at the sky.

Either the man standing in front of him belonged to a species that had gone out of the universe so long ago that not even the archives remembered it — dead before the archives, dead before the people who wrote them, dead in that first enormous silence before anything much had got started.

Or he was hiding. And hiding well. Well enough to stand three feet from a Time Lord and lie to the cleverest instrument in six galaxies without so much as changing his breathing.

Neither of those was a comfortable thing to be sharing a desert with.

"What's your name?"

"The answer is already hidden in your question."

The Doctor sighed. "Oh, don't do that. Don't be one of those. A person's name can't just be Mayor."

"Apparently yours can be Doctor."

The Doctor opened his mouth.

Nothing came out of it.

It was, he reflected afterwards, an extremely good piece of work. He had been doing that particular trick to other people since before this planet had a name, and had never once been on the receiving end of it, and it turned out to be considerably less charming from where he was now standing.

He looked away, out at the flat gold nothing, and let a moment go by.

"Right," he said.

"Where are you from?"

And the change in the man was so fast and so complete that the Doctor almost missed it — a shutter coming down behind the eyes, quicker than an expression, gone before it had properly finished being there.

"Where I come from doesn't matter."

He turned his face towards the horizon.

"What matters is where I am now."

It was said quietly. That was the part the Doctor kept, afterwards, and turned over, and did not like. Not shouted. Not bitten off. Just laid down flat, the way you say a thing you have said to yourself several thousand times in the dark until it stopped having edges.

There was something underneath it, though, and it was old, and it was cold, and after a moment the Doctor put a name to it and wished he hadn't.

Hatred.

Not of him. He knew that one; he'd been hated by professionals. Not of the desert, or the crack, or the situation.

The man hated where he was from. He hated his own kind, thoroughly and specifically, the way you can only hate something you were once part of — and the Doctor knew that particular flavour better than he knew most things, because he had carried a version of it himself for longer than he cared to count, in a box at the back, with a war inside it.

He looked at the Mayor with something that was not entirely suspicion any more.

He was drawing breath to ask the next question — the real one, the one underneath — when the light appeared.

It began as very little. A white point, out ahead of them, perhaps a hundred metres away — exactly a hundred metres away, the Doctor thought, exactly where he had been told — hanging at about the height of a man's chest with nothing holding it up.

Then it grew.

Slowly. That was the thing he noticed first and liked least. Cracks did not grow slowly; they tore, all at once, with a sound like the world being unstitched. This spread outward at a steady, unbothered rate, the way a curtain opens when somebody is pulling the cord hand over hand at the side of the stage.

The Doctor watched it widen. Two metres. Five. Ten. The white got into everything, flattening the sand, throwing two long shadows back across the ripples behind them, and still it came, until it stood in front of them the height of a cathedral door and then twice that and then more.

"Here we go," he said.

And then it went out.

Not closed. Not healed over. Went out, all at once, like a bulb — and where the light had been there was a shape in the desert that had not been in the desert thirty seconds earlier.

The Doctor stared at it.

Slowly, he lowered the sonic screwdriver, which he did not remember having raised.

This wasn't a crack.

"That's a cave," he said.

The Mayor said nothing.

"That's a cave," the Doctor said again, in a slightly different voice, as though the sentence might behave better the second time. It did not. It sat there in the air being exactly as wrong as it had been before.

Because a hundred metres away, where there had been flat sand all the way to a ruled horizon, there was now a hill. A great black shoulder of rock heaved up out of the desert with sand banked against its sides in long smooth drifts, as though it had been sitting there for ten thousand years minding its own business. And in the front of it, wide enough to drive a cathedral through, was a mouth.

"A very, very big cave," he added.

He should have been frightened. He noted, with the small detached part of him that kept minutes at moments like this, that he was not frightened, and that he ought to have a word with himself about that later.

The trouble was that a crack in space and time was a door out. And a cave was a door in. And something had promised him the first, at a hundred metres, in five minutes, and had then gone to considerable trouble to give him the second instead — which meant either the universe had made a clerical error, or somebody had changed the offer at the last moment, having decided that what the Doctor really wanted was not to leave at all.

He stared into the dark of the mouth. There was a great deal of it.

The grin arrived. He couldn't help it. It never had asked permission.

"Oh," he said, "this is going to be fun."

They walked to it, and it took no time at all, which was another thing he filed away and did not mention. A hundred metres should take a hundred and twenty paces. It took eleven.

Up close, the rock was not like rock. It had no grain to it, no seams, none of the long slow arguments between one layer and the next that a hillside spends a million years having with itself. It was simply black, and smooth, and cool under his palm when he laid a hand flat against it, and it was cool in a desert with light coming from everywhere, which meant that heat was going somewhere and he could not see where.

Inside the mouth there was almost no light at all. Three paces in, the daylight gave up entirely, as though it had been asked politely to stop at the door.

The Doctor thumbed the sonic screwdriver, and the little green lamp at its tip came up, and he stepped into the darkness.

The Mayor followed him.

For a while there was nothing but the sound of two sets of footsteps on a floor that was far too level, and the green light swinging.

The Doctor barely noticed any of it. That was the truth of it, and he'd have admitted it if anyone had asked — his curiosity had hold of him by the collar and was walking him deeper and deeper into the black at a pace that had left ordinary caution somewhere back near the entrance, along with the Mayor, who was now half a corridor behind and having to hurry.

Because something here was wrong, and it was wrong in a way he almost recognised, and almost was the single most dangerous word in his enormous vocabulary.

The cave had not appeared.

That was the thing his mind kept coming back and putting its hand on. It had not been made, either — not in front of him, not out of nothing. It had been revealed. The light hadn't built the hill; the light had switched off and let him see a hill that was already standing there, and had been standing there, quietly, for a very long time, in a desert with no wind and self-tidying sand and a horizon drawn with a ruler.

It had been waiting.

He was entirely certain of it, in the way he was certain of very few things, and the certainty had a shape to it he did not like at all: not an accident. Not a coincidence. A purpose, laid down long ago by somebody patient, and he had walked into the front of it grinning.

They went down.

The floor sloped, gently at first and then less gently, and the passage narrowed until the Doctor could touch both walls with his elbows out, and then it opened, and then it narrowed again. Loose stone shifted underfoot. Somewhere above and behind them a rock came down and struck and struck again and stopped.

Then silence.

Then another corridor, and it was the same corridor.

The same width. The same slope. The same three-cornered notch in the left-hand wall at shoulder height, and the same scatter of fallen stone lying in the same arrangement on the floor, and the same rock coming down somewhere behind them and striking twice and stopping.

The Doctor said nothing about it. He did, however, begin counting.

It happened four more times.

"Doctor," the Mayor said, from somewhere behind him.

"Mm."

"We've been here before."

"Yes."

"Which one?"

"All of them," the Doctor said, and did not slow down.

And then, without any warning at all, the passage ended, and they stepped out of the neck of it into a space so large that the little green lamp simply gave up — the light going out and out and finding nothing to land on, no wall, no ceiling, no far side. Just the sense, on the skin, of an enormous volume of air standing very still.

The Doctor stopped.

He took two steps out into it, holding the sonic up, turning.

And his foot caught on something.

He looked down.

And froze.

It was small. Smaller than a football, dull grey-green, sitting half-buried in the dust of the chamber floor at an angle that suggested it had rolled there, a long time ago, and had been left. There was a band of markings round its middle. The green light moved across them.

The Doctor's face changed.

The curiosity went out of it first — all of it, at once, like a room being emptied. Then the shock came, arriving all in one piece, his mouth opening slightly and staying that way.

And then something else, which Clara would have recognised instantly, and which almost nobody else alive had ever been permitted to see on him.

Fear.

"That's impossible," he said.

The Mayor came up beside him. "What's that?"

The Doctor didn't seem to hear. He crouched, slowly, the way you approach something that might be asleep.

"Th— this can't be..."

He put a hand out and stopped an inch short of it, and made himself finish the movement, and picked it up.

It weighed nothing. That was the first blasphemy. A thing that size, in the hand, ought to have some argument in it, and this had none — it sat in his palm like a held breath.

"It's impossible," he said again, quietly, and turned it over.

The markings ran round the middle in a circle, and the circle was made of circles, and the Doctor read them the way other men read a road sign, without effort, without wanting to.

"This," he said, "is a Time Lord lighting system."

The Mayor said nothing at all.

"They built these billions of years ago." He turned it in the green light. Something inside it turned with it, slightly out of time, a fraction late, like a compass needle settling. "Before the Citadel. Before the Academy. Before the looms, before the wars, before anybody had thought of a name to put on the front of any of it. Old, old, old technology." He was talking too fast now, the way he did when he was frightened and would rather be interesting. "Think of it as a balloon. That's all. A balloon that makes light — you let it go, and it goes up, and it decides how much light the place needs, and then it makes precisely that much for approximately eleven thousand years."

He looked up, into all that black.

"One of these could light a planet," he said. "One. The whole night side of a world, from pole to pole, for as long as anybody needed it."

Then he looked back down at the thing in his hand, and his voice went very flat.

"But it can't be here."

Because they were not in Gallifrey's sky. They were three hundred feet under a desert on a planet that had only had a name for as long as one man had been alive to give it one, on the wrong side of a crack in the universe, and there was no version of the last ten billion years in which one of these had any business lying in the dust of it like a dropped toy.

The object began to rise.

It went out of his open hand without being released — simply lifted, unhurried, as though it had been waiting for somebody to pick it up and put it the right way round. The Doctor stood, and looked up, and let it go.

It climbed. Ten feet. Thirty. A hundred, and still going, dwindling to a grey speck against nothing.

And then it lit.

There was no sound. That was somehow the worst of it. A white light came out of the thing above them and went everywhere at once — no flare, no build, no mercy — and the darkness did not retreat so much as cease to have ever been there, and the Doctor threw an arm across his eyes and lowered it slowly, blinking, and saw.

He turned round. All the way round, once, very slowly.

And understood.

It was not a cave.

The walls were not natural. Now that the light was on them they were obviously, insultingly not natural — dressed and squared and rising a hundred feet to a vaulted ceiling, laid in courses so fine he could not find a joint in them at four paces. There was a pattern worked into the stone at intervals along the whole enormous length, the same pattern, repeating: circles inside circles inside circles.

The corridors were not natural either. He could see them now, five of them, six, opening off the chamber at exact intervals around its curve, each one identical to the next, each one identical to the one they had come down.

They had not got lost in a cave system. They had been walked, patiently and by design, through the same corridor six times, by a building that had every intention of taking them exactly where it wanted them.

He turned to the last wall, and stopped.

It was not stone. It was a single sheet of something dull and seamless, forty feet across, and set into it at head height was a band of that same circular script, cut deep, and the Doctor read it and felt the floor of nine hundred years tilt very slightly under him.

This was not a chamber.

It was a cell.

Not a cell for anybody. It had been designed, from the ground up and at enormous expense, by people who understood exactly what they were building for — the shielding in the walls, the corridors that went nowhere and came back, the light that a prisoner could not put out, the absence of any way to tell how long you had been in it.

Built to hold a Time Lord.

Which raised, very quietly and all at once, a question the Doctor found he did not want to look directly at: not who had built it, nor when, nor why it was buried under a desert with no name at the bottom of a crack in the universe.

But whether it had ever been empty.

Then came the sound.

It was deep, and it was metallic, and it went through the soles of his shoes and up through both hearts — one enormous lock, somewhere in the structure of the place, turning over.

The chamber sealed itself.

The Doctor stood quite still in the middle of all that white light, in a room that had been made to hold precisely one of him, and did not turn round for a moment.

When he did, the Mayor was watching him.

He had not moved. He had not flinched at the lock, or the light, or the hundred-foot walls that had come out of nowhere.

He did not look surprised at all.

The Mayor snapped his fingers.

A time crack appeared, and the TARDIS emerged from it.The Doctor's TARDIS.

The Doctor stared at the Mayor in shock.

All of a sudden, the Sonic Screwdriver's distinctive sound echoed through the room.

The Doctor reached into his pocket and looked down at it. What he saw didn't actually surprise him that much. The scan he'd started ten minutes ago had suddenly completed for no apparent reason.

The Mayor's species was Time Lord.

The Sonic Screwdriver was Gallifreyan technology, after all. Now that it was surrounded by the right technology, it had finally started working properly.

The Doctor looked at the Mayor again, and suddenly everything fell into place.

The mysterious man standing before him was the Master.

“It's been a long time.”

“Yes, Doctor. A very, very long time.”

“Did you do all this just to lock me in a cell? I'm flattered. Though I am rather curious about what sort of trouble you're planning to get me into this time.”

The Doctor looked into the Master's face.

Anger. Hatred. Sadness.

He could see all of them at once.

But somehow, the Doctor knew none of it was meant for him. The Master was planning something else. Something much bigger.

“Doctor... I need your help.”

“What sort of help?”

“I need you to fight alongside me.”

“Against whom?”

The Master said nothing.

There was disappointment in his face.

“I think it would be better if I showed you.”

The Master snapped his fingers.

A time crack opened directly beneath the Doctor.

Before he could react, he fell into it.

The Doctor suddenly opened his eyes.

He immediately got to his feet and quickly scanned his surroundings.

It seemed to be a town of some sort.

His mind immediately filled with questions.

Skaro.

Clara's disappearance.

The anomalies.

And now the Master.

What was the connection between all of them?

The Master could apparently control the anomalies, but why had he been at the heart of Skaro?

Was that child really his?

Was Clara safe?

And why did the Master want the Doctor to fight alongside him?

Why hadn't he said who they were fighting?

He could have simply explained all of this instead of throwing the Doctor through a time crack.

The Doctor suddenly stopped himself.

Time Lords could control their minds in ways humans couldn't even begin to understand. Silencing his own thoughts was child's play to him.

Perhaps that was why, after everything he'd experienced over all those years, he hadn't completely lost his mind.

Maybe the secret was simply learning how to stop thinking.

The Doctor pushed those thoughts aside and began looking for clues about where he was.

It was dark.

Behind him came the enormous, rhythmic sound of waves crashing against the ocean.

He could feel the wind blowing towards the sea.

He looked around carefully.

Across the water, he could see a large collection of lights.

A settlement.

A town, perhaps.

There were people there. He was certain of that.

But on this enormous planet, it almost seemed as though this was the only place where anyone lived.

The Doctor didn't dwell on that for too long.

He set off towards the town.

For dozens of minutes, he tried to answer the questions racing through his mind.

Eventually, there were simply too many answers and not enough sense to hold them together.

So he stopped thinking.

There was only one thing he focused on now:

What was the Master trying to show me?

When the Doctor finally reached the town, he began hearing the voices of its inhabitants.

It was a warm, peaceful little place.

There were perhaps twenty houses, along with bars, restaurants and small areas where people gathered.

The Doctor reached the road that divided the town in two.

But something was strange.

Not a single person had made eye contact with him.

It was as though nobody could see him.

Nobody could even sense him.

The Doctor walked up to one of them.

“Hello.”

No response.

The people couldn't see him.

They couldn't even feel his presence.

And then—

that sound.

Perhaps the sound the Doctor had heard more times than any other in his life.

That familiar, crackling sound of the TARDIS materialising.

The Doctor stared.

He rarely saw the TARDIS materialise from the outside.

As it continued to take shape, he began noticing the differences.

This wasn't his TARDIS.

It was the Master's.

The TARDIS finished materialising.

The doors opened, and the Master stepped out.

But something was different about him.

The Doctor immediately sensed it.

The Master standing before him was much younger.

And, naturally, his face was different too.

The Doctor stared at him.

Then the TARDIS doors opened again.

A woman stepped out.

Tall.

Blonde.

Wearing a white dress.

The Doctor looked at the Master's face.

And suddenly, he understood.

This was before the Time War.

This was an old memory.

The Doctor wasn't simply witnessing the past.

He was inside the Master's memories.







































